course: dictionary
lesson: 13284
title: Gradient: steep slopes, graphs and colours
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one to use. «The steep gradient made the cyclist get off and push.» The clues are steep and push, so gradient means how steep a slope is.
explain: Gradient also has a meaning in maths. It is a number that shows how steep a line is on a graph. You can find it by dividing rise by run: how far up you go for each step across.
explain: Gradient can describe colour too. «The sky faded in a smooth gradient from pink to purple.» The words faded and smooth show that the colours change slowly, one into the next.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If «steep» fits the hill, the maths line or the colours, you have the right idea.
fact: «Gradient» means how steep a slope, hill or road is.
fact: In maths, gradient means rise ÷ run: how far up for each step across.
fact: Road signs can show a gradient as a percentage, such as 10%, which means 1 up for every 10 across.
fact: In art and computing, a gradient is a smooth change from one colour or shade to another.
exercise: «The road up the hill has a gradient of 1 in 10.» What does gradient mean here? | The colour of the road | Wide road | *How steep the road is | How many cars use the road
exercise: In maths, which sum finds the gradient of a line? | Rise × run | Run ÷ rise | Rise + run | *Rise ÷ run
exercise: What is a gradient in art? | A sharp line between two colours | *A smooth change from one colour to another | A picture of the sky | A type of paint brush
test: «The steep gradient made the cyclist get off and push.» What does gradient mean? | *How steep a slope is | How fast a car can go | How long a road is | A kind of cycle helmet
test: What does a gradient of 2 on a graph show? | A line made of two colours | *A line that rises 2 for every 1 across | A line that has no end | A line that is perfectly flat
exam: Which best describes a gradient of 1 in 10 on a road sign? | The road is 10 metres wide | For every 1 metre across, the road rises 10 metres | *For every 10 metres across, the road rises 1 metre | The road is 10 percent longer
